theory.intro:
A **logarithmic scale** measures a quantity by its order of magnitude, so small numbers describe an enormous range. On the illustrated positive base-$10$ log scale, each unit increase multiplies the quantity by ten. Related scales include the **Richter scale**, **pH** and **decibels**, but their steps work differently: a pH increase of one divides hydrogen-ion concentration by ten, and a decibel increase of ten multiplies sound intensity by ten.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.9,0) -- (6.6,0);
\foreach \x in {0,1.9,3.8,5.7} \draw (\x,-0.18) -- (\x,0.18);
\node[above] at (0,0.2) {$m$};
\node[above] at (1.9,0.2) {$m+1$};
\node[above] at (3.8,0.2) {$m+2$};
\node[above] at (5.7,0.2) {$m+3$};
\node[below] at (0,-0.22) {$A$};
\node[below] at (1.9,-0.22) {$10A$};
\node[below] at (3.8,-0.22) {$100A$};
\node[below] at (5.7,-0.22) {$1000A$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0.2,-1.25) -- (1.7,-1.25) node[midway, below] {$\times 10$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (2.1,-1.25) -- (3.6,-1.25) node[midway, below] {$\times 10$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (4.0,-1.25) -- (5.5,-1.25) node[midway, below] {$\times 10$};
\node[left] at (-1.0,0.45) {magnitude};
\node[left] at (-1.0,-0.55) {value};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
The chart shows the Richter magnitudes of three earthquakes. Using $A=10^M$, find how many times greater the ground amplitude at Harbour was than at Valley.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.8,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,7.6);
\foreach \y in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7} {\draw (-0.12,\y)--(0,\y); \path (-0.18,\y) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\y$};}
\draw (0.7,0) rectangle (1.7,6);
\draw (2.7,0) rectangle (3.7,3);
\draw (4.7,0) rectangle (5.7,5);
\path (1.2,0) node[below] {Harbour};
\path (3.2,0) node[below] {Valley};
\path (5.2,0) node[below] {Island};
\path (-0.95,3.8) node[rotate=90] {Richter magnitude};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

